.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_ASYNC 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_async \- submit an HTTP request for asynchronous execution
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ctpool_future *chttpclient_do_async(chttpcli cli,
                                    const chttp_request_t *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Non-blocking counterpart to chttpclient_do.  Queues req onto
chttpclient's shared, lazily-started reactor engine and returns
immediately; the whole request/response cycle, including any
redirect hops, runs on the engine's own threads.

The engine (a small pool of reactor threads plus a companion
DNS/connect worker pool, both sized to the CPU count) starts on the
first call to chttpclient_do_async or chttpclient_do_async_streaming
anywhere in the process and stops automatically once no request is
in flight and no connection remains in any chttpcli's async idle
pool.  It is entirely separate from chttpclient_do's synchronous
connection handling.  This engine is independent of chttpserver's own
event_loop-based reactor; a process may freely run chttpserver and
chttpclient_do_async/_streaming at the same time, and stopping either
one's engine (chttpsvr_engine_stop(), or this engine's own automatic
teardown) has no effect on the other.

Unlike chttpclient_do, req need not remain valid after this call
returns; everything needed is copied or serialised internally
before the call returns.

Any interim informational (1xx) response the server sends is
transparently discarded, and reading continues on the same
connection for the real response; the future is never fulfilled
with a 1xx status as though it were the final answer. RFC 8297 "103
Early Hints" is the most common real-world example. A request with
req->expect_continue set (see chttp_request_t's own doc comment)
sends "Expect: 100-continue" and withholds the body until the
server's interim response, exactly like chttpclient_do; the wait is
implemented as part of this function's own non-blocking dispatch
rather than a blocking call. A server that never stops sending
interim responses is not tolerated indefinitely: after 64
consecutive discarded interim responses on one hop, the future is
fulfilled with ccol_http_transfer_aborted instead of waiting
forever. This cap applies independently to the wait for "100
Continue" and to the final response read that follows it: a "100
Continue" (or the wait simply timing out) is itself a genuine,
non-discarded outcome, so it resets the count; a hop using
req->expect_continue may therefore discard up to 64 interim
responses while waiting for "100 Continue" and up to 64 more while
reading the final response, not merely 64 combined.

A request header (or, on a request with a body, body.content_type)
containing an embedded CR or LF byte, or a body-carrying request
(POST, PUT, or PATCH) whose caller-set Content-Length header is not
a plain unsigned decimal digit string (no leading '+'/'-', no
embedded whitespace) exactly matching the actual body length, is a
distinct case: it can
only be detected once the request is actually serialised for the
wire, which happens after the future already exists (unlike a
malformed URL or unusable TLS config, both checked up front). Such a
request still returns a non-NULL future; its eventual result (see
chttpclient_async_result_get(3)) carries rv == ccol_invalid_args
instead of a successful response, exactly the same as
chttpclient_do would return synchronously for the equivalent
request; see chttp_request_set_header(3) and chttpclient_do(3) for
why this is rejected.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli Client handle; must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req Request to execute; must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A future, or NULL if the request could not even be queued (NULL req,
cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle,
malformed URL, TLS unusable, OOM, or the engine failing to start).
On success, the caller owns the future and must eventually
call chttpclient_async_result_free (after
chttpclient_async_result_get) followed by exactly one
ctpool_future_free.
.fi
